      en: "Tonle Sap Lake is the largest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia and a biological hotspot of immense global importance. Its defining characteristic is a unique hydrological phenomenon where the Tonle Sap River reverses its flow during the monsoon season, channeling water from the Mekong into the lake basin. This causes the lake's surface area to expand dramatically from about 2,500 square kilometers in the dry season to over 16,000 square kilometers at its peak. Designated as a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve in 1997, it supports an extraordinary array of biodiversity, including over 200 fish species and numerous endangered waterbirds. The seasonal flood pulse powers one of the most productive inland fisheries in the world, supplying roughly 75% of Cambodia's annual protein intake. Historically, the lake's resources were the foundation of the Angkorian civilization, providing the agricultural surplus needed for empire-building. Today, tens of thousands of people live in floating villages, their livelihoods intricately tied to the lake's cyclical nature. Sustaining this fragile ecosystem is critical for Cambodia's food security and environmental heritage.",
